# shir_nodes/regexp_find.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# RegexpFind: LEFTMOST regex match as a string (Go
# regexp.MustCompile(pat) + re.FindString(s); sed/grep -o family).
#
# GENERIC (any frontend whose source language has regex literals).
# text is any expression evaluating to a string; pattern is the regex
# source (dialect: ERE/RE2-class — char classes, anchors, quantifiers,
# groups). Yields "" when nothing matches.
#
# Backends: JS (TEXT.match(new RegExp(PAT)) || [""])[0],
#           Perl do { my $m; ($T =~ /($P)/) && ($m = $1); $m // "" },
#           Rust reg.find(), C regexec loop

node RegexpFind
tag "RegexpFind"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field pattern: expr
# Spec: TextOps#regexp_find_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy, section
# "RegexpFind: the leftmost match, as a string"). Stated over an ABSTRACT matcher that
# reports the leftmost match's DECOMPOSITION (prefix, matched text, suffix), or None —
# the node's value is that middle component, and [] for None. Proved: no match yields
# ""; a reported match yields exactly the reported bytes (the node never re-derives,
# re-encodes or normalises them); "" comes back exactly when there is no match or the
# match is empty; and for any matcher reporting a GENUINE decomposition the returned
# bytes occur in the subject (`s = p @ result @ q`), which is what makes a
# take/drop-based rendering sound.
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): leftmost-ness itself (the abstraction hands back
# the leftmost match, so an engine that skipped an earlier match is not caught — search
# order, alternation preference and greediness are the engine's contract); the regex
# DIALECT (ERE/RE2 classes, anchors, quantifiers, groups), case folding, locale and
# Unicode rules; capture groups and backreferences, which are not this node's value;
# evaluation of `text`/`pattern` (the model takes already-evaluated bytes); and bytes
# only — no encoding or Unicode normalisation.
spec: TextOps#regexp_find_sound
